  "body": "Part of #1490.\n\nThe 2D rest generator refuses Adaptive by name. The current adaptive planner starts with a full pocket and its own entry bore.\n\nImplement Adaptive over the material that earlier operations leave. Keep the existing refusal until the required stock and entry proofs are available.\n\nDesign decision:\n- Give the planner a separate legal cutter-center region and conservative remaining-material region at each cut depth.\n- Derive cleared space from verified previous-operation stock. Do not treat an entered previous diameter as proof that material is absent.\n- Initialize engagement from remaining material. Do not initialize it from the whole pocket or assume that the entry bore exists.\n- Prove the whole cutter footprint and approach volume clear before using a cleared-space entry or rapid.\n- Refuse uncertain stock, missing cut depth, and an entry with no proved clearance by name.\n- Preserve the conservative remaining-material bound when rasterizing. Uncertain cells contain material.\n- Keep the bounded planner work budget and named refusal when the load or coverage cannot be proved.\n\nTests:\n- Use a rounded pocket with four corner residues after a larger tool.\n- Measure engagement against simulated previous cuts at every requested depth.\n- Cover islands, disconnected rest areas, narrow necks, and a roughing pass that stops above the rest floor.\n- Prove no rapid meets material, every emitted path passes verification, and reachable rest material is covered.\n- Remove each stock, depth, and entry guard separately. Each mutation must fail its pinning test.\n\nDone means: Adaptive runs on proved remaining stock, passes these tests, and refuses unsupported stock or entry cases by name.\n\nRelated work: the separate #1490 tasks for carried-stock input and entry from cleared space supply the required proofs. This task adds the adaptive engagement plan.\n\nLiterature: M. Held, On the Computational Geometry of Pocket Machining, LNCS 500 (1991), https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/3-540-54103-9. Z. Yao, S. K. Gupta and D. S. Nau, Algorithms for selecting cutters in multi-part milling problems, Computer-Aided Design 35 (2003), https://www.cs.umd.edu/~nau/papers/yao2003algorithms.pdf.\n\nThese sources describe pocket geometry and cutter coverage. The clearance and stock-proof contract above is this project's decision. Do not copy another CAM product's code or output.\n",
